      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the volume of each cube and then find the ratio of the volume of the smaller cube to the volume of the larger cube.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches. The formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a cube is given by:\n\n\\[\nV = s^3\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( s \\) is the edge length of the cube. For the cube with an edge length of six inches:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{small}} = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot. We need to convert one foot to inches first:\n\n\\[\n1 \\text{ foot} = 12 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nSo, the volume of the larger cube with an edge length of one foot is:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{large}} = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the ratio of the volume of the smaller cube to the volume of the larger cube. This ratio is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_{\\text{small}}}{V_{\\text{large}}} = \\frac{216}{1728}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. The GCD of 216 and 1728 is 216. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 216:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{216}{1728} = \\frac{216 \\div 216}{1728 \\div 216} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the volume of the smaller cube to the volume of the larger cube is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\n\\]",
