      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of one foot, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches.\n2. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot.\n3. Find the ratio of the two volumes.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches. The formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a cube with edge length \\( s \\) is:\n\\[\nV = s^3\n\\]\nFor a cube with an edge length of six inches, the volume is:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{6 inches}} = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot. Since one foot is equal to 12 inches, the edge length in inches is 12. The volume is:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{1 foot}} = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot:\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_{\\text{6 inches}}}{V_{\\text{1 foot}}} = \\frac{216}{1728}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. The GCD of 216 and 1728 is 216. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 216:\n\\[\n\\frac{216}{1728} = \\frac{216 \\div 216}{1728 \\div 216} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of one foot is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\n\\]",
